    "What is your relationship with mathematics?": "I’ve always found math a little intimidating, mostly because of how abstract it can get. During lower secondary school, I struggled with topics like algebra and geometry. I think I just didn’t care enough to really focus. Now that I'm unemployed and home with my two kids, I've started trying to understand it more, especially for cooking measurements and budgeting.",
    "Do you ever get anxious when thinking about mathematics?": "Yes, occasionally. When I'm trying to figure out something complicated, like how much to spend on groceries or how to split a bill with my kids, my brain starts to race. It's not crippling, but it makes me feel a bit overwhelmed. I think it's more about not having enough confidence than the actual math itself.",
    "Did you ever use AI to support your math learning in the last year? If yes, how was your experience?": "I used an AI app once to help with fractions and percentages. I was cooking and needed to scale a recipe, so I typed in the original amount and the new serving size, and it gave me the adjusted measurements. It was helpful, but it didn’t really explain the concept behind it. I preferred to figure it out on my own, even if it took longer.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to solve a second order algebraic equation?": "First, make sure the equation is in standard form: ax² + bx + c = 0. Then, use the quadratic formula: x equals negative b plus or minus the square root of b² minus 4ac, all over 2a. Plug in the values for a, b, and c. Calculate the discriminant. If it's positive, you get two real solutions. If zero, one solution. If negative, complex solutions.",
    "How would you explain, step by step, how to find the stationary points of an equation y=f(x)?": "To find stationary points, take the derivative of the function, f'(x). Then, set f'(x) equal to zero and solve for x. These x-values are the locations of the stationary points. Plug them back into the original function to find the corresponding y-values. These points are where the function stops increasing or decreasing.",
